From: serlk <22211586+Serlk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:22:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 02/34] media: mediatek: camsv: Add MT6878 CAMSV capture driver

A V4L2 capture driver for the CAMSV engine of MT6878. One engine, one tag
(tag 2, the plain RAW image), one video node with a media sink pad; the
source (a seninf subdev) is linked through the DT graph when its driver
binds, and its stream is enabled with the capture.

The command queue (CQ) builder writes 12-byte little-endian descriptors
(control word plus an unaligned 64-bit value pointer) from the start of a
DMA area and the written values from its end; long writes are split into
511-word chunks, address/value pair lists into runs of consecutive
registers, lists end with an END descriptor and are aligned to 16 bytes.
On top of it the per-tag configuration and frame commands, the common
channel configuration and the tag grouping are composed. The builder is
pure data code with a safe profile that rejects what the descriptor
format cannot express, and a failed composition never changes the
session. KUnit tests cover the encoding, the space accounting, the
alignment, a full first-frame composition and the rejections.

Every buffer is one job: its CQ (configuration on the first job, the
output address and commit on every job) is composed into a coherent
slot, bound to the job's cookie and launched. The binding is released at
the start of frame whose active cookie matches, and only then is the next
job launched, so at most one set of outer settings is pending. The four
IRQ handlers take a snapshot under one raw spinlock and queue it in a
kfifo; their common threaded handler drains it as the single consumer,
runs the cookie automaton and returns buffers with vb2. Reset errors are
reported: a timeout fails the runtime resume, and on stream off it marks
the engine wedged since its DMA may still be running. The engine is used
behind the MM IOMMU only. There is no SMI clamp in mainline, and no
QoS/DVFS; DMA FIFO thresholds use the MT6878 values of the ISP7SP driver.

Based on mtk_cam-sv.c, mtk_cam-sv-regs.h, mtk_cam-engine*.h and
mtk_cam-plat-mt6878.c of MediaTek's ISP7SP camsys driver (GPL-2.0), with
the command queue and the engine handling written from a clean-room
behavioural specification; no code of the factory firmware was used.

